GHES Alerting Runbook — Graphite / collectd → Grafana → Teams

Reference for setting up alert rules by hand in the GHES Management Console's built-in Grafana (3.18+), or in an external Grafana pointed at the same data.

Before anything else

Replace HOST in every query with your literal appliance hostname (e.g. ghes-01).

Do not use $host or any dashboard template variable. Alert rules cannot resolve them. A rule containing $host will create without error and then never fire — the single most common way this goes wrong.

Validate every query first. Paste it into a panel in the Management Console's Grafana and confirm it returns a number. The paths below use the standard collectd namespace, but yours may differ by collectd and GHES version. If a query returns nothing, browse the metric tree in the query editor and fix the path before building the rule.

Quick reference
#	Alert	Severity	Reducer	Op	Threshold	Pending	Lookback
1	Data volume free %	Critical	last	<	15%	10m	15m
2	Data volume free % (early warn)	Warning	last	<	25%	30m	1h
3	Root volume free %	Critical	last	<	10%	10m	15m
4	Memory used %	Warning	mean	>	90%	15m	15m
5	Swap in use	Warning	mean	>	2 GB	30m	30m
6	CPU iowait %	Warning	mean	>	25%	15m	15m
7	Load average (5m)	Warning	mean	>	1.5 × cores	15m	15m
8	CPU idle %	Info	mean	<	15%	20m	20m

In Default mode, the Reducer column goes in the When field and the Op + Threshold columns go in the is above / is below condition. See the setup walkthrough below.

Severity is a label on each rule. In Default mode it's cosmetic — you pick the contact point directly. In Advanced mode it's what notification policies route on.

Roll out in this order

Do not enable all eight on day one. You will get noise, mute the channel, and lose the value of the whole exercise.

Week 1 — alerts 1 and 3 only. Disk is what actually takes GHES down. Live with just these and confirm they stay quiet.
Week 2 — add 2, 4, 5. Capacity trend and memory pressure.
Week 3 — add 6, 7, 8 if you still want them. Many teams find these redundant once disk and memory are covered.

Each week, if something fired and it wasn't actionable, fix the threshold before adding more.

Setup walkthrough
One-time: contact point

Alerting → Contact points → Add contact point

Name: teams-platform-ops
Integration: Webhook (predictable payload you shape in Power Automate) or Microsoft Teams (emits adaptive-card-shaped JSON)
URL: your Power Automate workflow URL
Test — confirm it lands in Teams before building any rules

Treat that URL as a credential. Anyone holding it can post to your channel.

Default vs Advanced mode

The "Define query and alert condition" step has a Default / Advanced toggle. Every alert in this runbook is a single query with one reducer and one threshold, which is exactly what Default covers. Use Default.

The two modes also apply to notifications: Default routes directly to a contact point, Advanced routes via notification policies. Default is simpler and removes a whole class of silent-failure.

You can switch modes at any time, but going Advanced → Default may hit queries that can't be converted and Grafana will warn before resetting. Pick one mode per rule and stay there.

If your Grafana doesn't show the toggle, the feature isn't enabled on that instance — use the Advanced fallback further down.

Per rule — Default mode

Alerting → Alert rules → New alert rule

Name — from the table above
Data source — your Graphite datasource
Query — switch to Code mode (not Builder), paste the query
When — the reducer from the table (last / mean)
is above / is below — the operator and threshold from the table
Folder — GHES Platform Alerts (create once, reuse)
Evaluation group — ghes-capacity, interval 1m
Pending period — from the table
Contact point — teams-platform-ops, selected right on the rule
Labels — severity=critical|warning, source=ghes. Not required for delivery in Default mode, but useful in the alert list and free to add
Annotations — summary and description (see each alert below; this text is what lands in Teams, so make it useful at 3am)

How the table maps to the form:

Runbook column	Default mode field
Query	the query box, Code mode
Reducer	When
Op + Threshold	is above / is below + value
Pending	Set evaluation behavior → Pending period
Fallback — Advanced mode

Only if the Default toggle isn't available, or you need multiple queries or math expressions. Advanced exposes the pipeline Default builds for you:

Stage	refId	What it does
Query	A	Runs the Graphite query
Reduce	B	Collapses the series to one value
Threshold	C	Compares to threshold — this is the condition

Build A → B (Reduce, input A) → C (Threshold, input B), then set C as the alert condition. B and C are expressions, not datasource queries.

In Advanced mode notifications route by notification policy, so you also need: Alerting → Notification policies → child policy matching source = ghes → route to teams-platform-ops. Skip that and rules fire into the default contact point instead — silent failure, everything looks green.

The alerts
1 & 2 — Data volume free %

The one that matters. A full /data/user puts GHES into read-only. Users cannot push. This is the outage people wish they had alerted on.

asPercent(collectd.HOST.df-data-user.df_complex-free,
          sumSeries(collectd.HOST.df-data-user.df_complex-*))

Two rules off the same query:

	Early warning	Critical
Threshold	< 25%	< 15%
Pending	30m	10m
Severity	warning	critical

The early warning exists so someone can plan; the critical exists so someone wakes up. Two tiers off one query is cheap and much better than one threshold that is either too twitchy or too late.

Annotation description:

Data volume free space low. GHES enters read-only at zero — users cannot push. Check repository growth, Actions artifacts, old backups, and /data/user/tmp. Run ghe-storage-extend if expansion is needed.

Mount name check: df-data-user maps to /data/user. If empty, run the discovery query to see real mount names:

aliasByNode(collectd.HOST.df-*.df_complex-free, 2)
3 — Root volume free %

Root filling is almost always runaway logs.

asPercent(collectd.HOST.df-root.df_complex-free,
          sumSeries(collectd.HOST.df-root.df_complex-*))

< 10%, pending 10m, severity critical.

Annotation description:

Root volume low — usually runaway logs. Check /var/log, journal size, and failed job output. Distinct from the data volume; this fills faster and with less warning.

4 — Memory used %
asPercent(collectd.HOST.memory.memory-used,
          sumSeries(collectd.HOST.memory.memory-*))

> 90%, reducer mean, pending 15m, severity warning.

Use mean not last — memory spikes constantly and last will page you for a single scrape.

Why this denominator: memory-used excludes cached and buffered, so this reflects real pressure. Raw "used" on Linux includes page cache, looks alarming, and means nothing.

5 — Swap in use
scale(collectd.HOST.swap.swap-used, 0.000000001)

> 2 (GB), reducer mean, pending 30m, severity warning.

The long pending period is deliberate. Transient swap during backups is normal. Sustained swap is what precedes user-visible slowness.

6 — CPU iowait %

Underrated for GHES. Git operations are IO-heavy, and iowait climbs before users start complaining about slow clones.

averageSeries(collectd.HOST.cpu-*.cpu-wait)

> 25%, reducer mean, pending 15m, severity warning.

If empty, your collectd may use the aggregation plugin instead:

collectd.HOST.aggregation-cpu-average.cpu-wait
7 — Load average (5 minute)
collectd.HOST.load.load.midterm

Threshold is core-count dependent. Rule of thumb: warn at 1.5 × cores.

Cores	Warn	Critical
4	6	12
8	12	24
16	24	48
32	48	96

Use midterm (5 min), not shortterm — shortterm spikes constantly.

8 — CPU idle %
averageSeries(collectd.HOST.cpu-*.cpu-idle)

< 15%, pending 20m, severity info.

Largely redundant with load average. Include only if you find it useful; drop it if it duplicates alert 7.

Application-level alerts

The [collectd] System & Application Insights dashboard has GHES-specific metrics — job queues, git operations, search, and so on. I am not listing paths for those because they vary meaningfully across GHES releases and I would be guessing.

Get them the reliable way: open that dashboard, edit any panel you care about, and copy the target string out of the query editor. Those queries are already correct for your version.

Worth looking for:

Job/queue depth (backed-up queues precede visible slowness)
Failed job counts
Git operation rates and error rates
Search/indexing health

Same three-stage pattern applies — only the query changes.

Tuning

Set the time range to 30 days before choosing any threshold. Look at how the metric actually behaves. Disk trends slowly and smoothly. Load spikes many times a day. Those want very different pending periods, and picking numbers without looking is how you end up with a muted channel.

Pending period is your main noise control. A threshold that trips for 90 seconds during a backup is not an incident. Raising pending from 5m to 20m usually eliminates more false positives than changing the threshold.

mean for volatile signals, last for slow ones. Disk uses last because it moves slowly and you want the current value. Memory, CPU, and load use mean because a single sample is meaningless.

Review after two weeks. For each alert that fired, ask: did anyone do anything? If no, the threshold is wrong or the alert should not exist.

Testing each rule
Temporarily set the threshold so it fires immediately — e.g. disk free < 99%
Save, wait one evaluation interval
Confirm the rule shows Firing in Alerting → Alert rules
Confirm the message arrives in Teams
Restore the real threshold
Confirm it returns to Normal

Do this for the first rule you build. Once the path is proven, you can trust subsequent rules without testing each one.

Common failure modes
Symptom	Cause
Rule never fires	$host left in the query, or the metric path is wrong
Rule fires, nothing in Teams	Default mode: no contact point selected on the rule. Advanced mode: notification policy not routing the labels
Fires constantly	Pending period too short, or last where mean belongs
No Data state	Query returns nothing — validate it in a panel
Fires on restart/backup	Expected; raise the pending period
Message unreadable in Teams	Annotations empty — populate summary and description
If you later want this as code

The Ansible role covers the same rules against Grafana's provisioning API. Note the caveat in its README: there is no documented API for the built-in Grafana, so the automated path is only fully supported against an external Grafana instance. Clicking these in once is a legitimate choice.
